 tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "The DOWN action had gameplay_changed: true. Let me check the current state - I can see a red block appeared in the image! Let me verify.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Check current state\nfor n in current_frame.segmentation['nodes']:\n    if n['color'] not in ['w', 'O']:\n        b = n['boundary']\n        rows = [p[0] for p in b]\n        cols = [p[1] for p in b]\n        print(f\"Color {n['color']}: bbox=({min(rows)},{min(cols)})-({max(rows)},{max(cols)}), pixels={n['pixels']}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "Color Y: bbox=(4,12)-(11,23), pixels=80\nColor Y: bbox=(4,28)-(11,39), pixels=80\nColor Y: bbox=(4,44)-(11,55), pixels=80\nColor R: bbox=(16,8)-(19,19), pixels=48\nColor b: bbox=(20,8)-(23,27), pixels=80\nColor M: bbox=(56,40)-(59,43), pixels=16\nColor c: bbox=(60,40)-(62,43), pixels=12\nColor W: bbox=(63,0)-(63,29), pixels=30\nColor N: bbox=(63,30)-(63,63), pixels=34", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "Interesting! After DOWN:\n- Red block 1 reappeared at (16,8)-(19,19), 4x12\n- Blue bar moved to (20,8)-(23,27), 4x20\nSo the DOWN action moved the blue bar down by 1 row (from row 16-19 to row 20-23), and the red block reappeared at (16,8)-(19,19).\nWait, but the blue bar was at (16,8)-(19,27) before, and now it's at (20,8)-(23,27). So the blue bar moved down by 4 rows (from row 16 to row 20).\nAnd the red block reappeared at (16,8)-(19,19), which is where the blue bar was before.\nSo the mechanic is:\n- When the blue bar moves, it leaves behind a red block at its previous position (or part of it).\n- The blue bar moves in steps of 4 rows or 4 columns.\nThis explains why the UP a